<edge source="global_money_markets::market_volatility" target="brazil_market_turmoil::market_volatility" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.9</data>
  <data key="d36">Spikes in global volatility trigger synchronized mark‑to‑market losses, margin calls and risk‑off trading that translate directly into larger intraday swings and higher realized/implied volatility in Brazilian equity, bond and FX markets.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-3 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_money_markets</data>
  <data key="d39">brazil_market_turmoil</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_money_markets::market_volatility" target="iran_regional_escalation::energy_price_volatility" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.85</data>
  <data key="d36">Global equity and FX volatility forces cross‑asset deleveraging and margin calls that cascade into commodity markets, producing larger intraday and weekly moves in energy contracts.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_money_markets</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_regional_escalation</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_money_markets::market_volatility" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::regulatory_scrutiny_inte" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Spikes in market volatility—especially in tech and AI equities—trigger faster regulatory inquiries and precautionary rulemaking aimed at investor protection and systemic risk, increasing the intensity of AI-related regulatory scrutiny.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_money_markets</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
